Z||000009||007DOUG||03-31-2000||01:48 AM||||About a year ago,  I contacted Bell Tech about making a large run of spindles. 1,000 SETS to be exact. I was inquiring as an individual, not as the "Fiero Warehouse". They could care less who I was, or how many I wanted. <P>Most companies still look at the fiero as a mistake. Few are interested in manufacturing ANYTHING fiero related.<P>But if you look at it from their point of view (money, money, money) you can see why.<P>They would plunk a ton of time & money into a design for a car that was made for only 5 years (actually 4 years considering the 88 version would have to be designed totally different), and as those years went on, fewer and fewer were produced. To them, the Fiero is a narrow market. Narrow compared to the hundreds of millions of potential lowrider compact/full size trucks sold each year. <P>Plus consider that most owners don't want to lower their car 2". The 1" springs are easier to produce and install. And your ride quality really doesn't suffer at all.<P>As manufacturers would say........"Its not in our best interest" or " Not economically feasible"<P>I REALLY hope something happens here. You can count me in for at least 5 sets.<P>But untill then, everyone, KEEP LOOKING. they are out there. I looked for three years before I found my set. Check your local speed shops and swap meets.<P>And when you do find them, be prepared to pay through the nose for them (mine came from ebay......Ouch!!)<BR>                                             ............Doug||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||03-31-2000||02:33 AM||||If they weren't too expensive, I'd be interested in buying a set next year.. maybe for a 1" or 1.5" drop.<P>I don't have my Fiero yet (*sigh*) but, I just thought I'd express my interest in this product.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodney||03-31-2000||06:08 PM||||Called around today. To the spindle place and Bell Tech. Bell Tech says it will not make them again. Period. Talked to upper management. Looks like they will never be done again. The spindle place said if I could buy the enginering drawings from Bell Tech it would be easier but Bell Tech says no. To start from scratch is a lot of effort and not worth it for a few sales. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||03-31-2000||09:48 PM||||Bummer!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I wonder what constitutes "only a few sales"? If they advertise: " Just let us know what you're looking for, and if we don't have it, we can manufacture it for you! " I wonder how they would balk at the possibility of more than one sale?<P>Like I said, it's hard to believe that we are having this many problems find these things.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Anyway, thanks for all the effort, guys.<P>Roy<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000013||Rodney||03-31-2000||10:36 PM||||He said about 200 pair a year for 5 years is a minimum amount to make it worth while. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||03-31-2000||11:50 PM||||There's always the welded in,Cadillac balljoints,giving a 1-1/2" dropped spindle effect.At $65 each,plus (TIG)welding it seemed like a decent alternative to me,so I did it.<BR>Now my roll center is just like stock,the car is lower,geometry is correct,and the ride is as good as it could be with the spring rate I have.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||04-01-2000||08:24 AM||||hey lowCG,<P>I'e never heard of Cadillac ball joint conversion before. Can you explain or give a URL to site so I can do some researh? Maybe we should start another thread on this topic.<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000016||Rodney||04-01-2000||02:22 PM||||These guys make a 2" drop spindle. Possibly use a 1/4" shim under a coil to make it a 1 1/2" drop. Or maybe they can be talked into making a different spindle? <BR> <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/</A> <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000017||lowCG||04-01-2000||05:59 PM||||Well I've done this on two cars now,with one failed attempt due to the use of a stick welder instead of TIG welding the parts into place.There are rubber parts inside balljoints,and care needs to be taken to cool the parts down right after welding a third of the way around the joint,after tack welding them into place,using a spray of water from a hose nozzle(NOT submersing).<BR>The parts to use that have the correct taper to fit our spindles come stock on mid seventies Cadillac,full sized cars,as upper units.Used as lower units gives around 1-1/2" of upward shift.The upper control arm is then pushed upward to a flatter angle,reducing bumpsteer a bit.The holes in the upper arms need to be slotted to get the car back into alignment.Also,the Caddy balljoints are much larger than stock,reducing wear,and stress/bind.<BR>Oh,and you might experience a scraping of the inner wheel lip at full lock after this,like with lowered spindles,so some minor grinding of the lower control arm may be needed.<BR>That's all there is to it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Spyder||04-04-2000||04:54 PM||||LowCG<BR>Does this sound like the right ball joints?<BR>What brand ball joint did you use?<BR>These all use the same ball joints:<BR>1975 Cadillac Fleetwood Brougham Talisman Sedan 4D<BR>1975 Cadillac DeVille Sedan 4D<BR>1975 Cadillac Calais Hardtop 4D<BR>1975 Cadillac DeVille Coupe 2D<P>PERFECT CIRCLE <BR>BALL JOINT ; Right Upper; <BR>260-1076 <BR>$137.86 <BR>$68.93<P>PERFECT CIRCLE <BR>BALL JOINT ; Left Upper <BR>260-1077 <BR>$137.86 <BR>$68.93 ||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||04-04-2000||11:35 PM||||Uh,ouch,yeah,bringing back memories now.<BR>Those applications should work fine,however,I used Moog parts at around the same cost when I did this last summer.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||william_thorniii||04-06-2000||01:27 AM||||CG,<P>Let's test my memory... are you in the Detriot area?  If so, I might be heading up to Warren soon and sure would love to check your suspension first hand before I tear some things up trying to reproduce what you have so easily done.  <P>Is there any particular advice that you can dispense about this swap?  Usually AFTER I finish a project, I find out a technique that would have saved me much time/grief/etc.  Your response is greatly appreciated.   Although I do wonder why I haven't heard of this earlier?<P>William T. Thorn, III  ||||reg||14
Z||000021||TwisT||04-08-2000||07:29 PM||||I'm curious for more info also.  Question,  does this mod only work for pre-'88 Fieros?||||reg||1
Z||000022||Spyder||04-08-2000||09:30 PM||||I will be ordering the parts Monday and then go from there.  I will let you guys know and with pics.||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||04-09-2000||09:23 AM||||Well,I pretty much covered it in the other post,the only other related issue would be establishing your ride height,and understanding that the front suspension will usually settle around 3/8" at the wheel after reassembly,and with lowering springs you may well need to raise the suspension a bit,as a "funkytoolow" situation will degrade any handling benefits gained.A spacer can be placed over the horn that the top of the spring goes over,using the stock,rubber pad.<BR>Because of the location of the spring in the whole suspension,a 5/8" spacer will give a 1" lift at the wheel(5/16":1/2",and so on).<P>A good quality thick steel washer may be needed under the balljoint hold-down nut,my first car needed this,however,my car that I have now didn't as I recall.I wouldn't recomend stacking up thin washers however.<P>I would highly recommend finding a professional welder for this job,who's confident in their skills.<P>I can probably provide a couple low quality pictures,as I have a dime store digital camera now,and the car is in my shop for the 3.4TDC,big brake swap anyhow.||||reg||7
Z||000024||lowCG||04-09-2000||12:08 PM||||I haven't had any experience with anything but the early cars,don't know about the newer ones,and I've never seen this type of construction done anywhere outside of racing,and not on Fiero's until I figured it out one day back in '93 by sorting through a wharehouse after the joints that the Pontiac dealer sold me turned out to be junk(studs turned in the ball,became sloppy after a few weeks).<BR>WT,e-mail me as to when you'll be in the area,I'll show you my Frankenstein suspension.(jrhansen@visitus.com)<BR>BTW,I posted a link to a couple pics on the Technical side of the forum.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 04-09-2000).]||||reg||1
